# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/spotlight_legend.grim
# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after "="; keep {tokens}.
# "# en:" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;
# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.


@event spot_tavern_song
# en: The Ballad of {player}
title [05748c] = {player}之歌
# en: The Newt has a new song, and it's about you. It has nine verses and a chorus everybody knows. In verse three you fight a frost giant on the lake. In verse six you marry a mermaid. The fiddler sees you in the doorway, bows, and starts again from the top, louder.
text [4519d3] = 蝾螈酒馆有了一首新歌，唱的是你。一共九段，副歌人人会唱。第三段里你在湖上大战冰霜巨人。第六段里你娶了一条美人鱼。拉小提琴的人看见你站在门口，鞠了一躬，从头又唱了一遍，声音更响了。
# en: A packed tavern, a fiddler standing on a bench, the whole room singing along with raised mugs, one student by the door with their face in their hands.
scene [9f912b] = 挤满人的酒馆里，一个小提琴手站在长凳上，满屋子的人举着杯子跟着唱，门边一个学生双手捂着脸。
# en: Teach them the true verses
opt.left.label [d96461] = 教他们唱真实的段落
# en: You climb on a bench and sing them what really happened, which rhymes much worse. The room listens politely, then goes back to the mermaid. But the fiddler keeps one of your verses, the true one about the night you got lost, and it's the one that makes people quiet.
opt.left.say.1 [653fba] = 你爬上长凳，把真实发生的事唱给他们听，押韵差得多。满屋子的人礼貌地听完，又回去唱美人鱼了。不过小提琴手留下了你的一段，那段真实的，关于你迷路的那个夜晚，每次唱到那里，大家都会安静下来。
# en: Let the legend grow
opt.right.label [0388a5] = 让传说越传越大
# en: By the next week the song has a tenth verse, in which you are cruel to a friend, by name. It isn't true. It is very catchy. The friend hears it at breakfast, and you spend a long evening putting it right.
opt.right.say.1 [ad2b40] = 到下个星期，这首歌多了第十段，唱你对一个朋友很刻薄，还点了名。那不是真的。可它非常上口。那位朋友在早餐时听到了，你花了一整个晚上才把事情弥补过来。
# en: You buy the fiddler a drink and let it grow. By spring there are fourteen verses, two dances and a version for children. You have never fought a frost giant. Half the village is sure you did.
opt.right.say.2 [be67bb] = 你请小提琴手喝了一杯，让歌继续长下去。到了春天，它已经有十四段、两支舞，还有一个儿童版。你从没跟冰霜巨人打过架。半个村子都确信你打过。

@event spot_witness_promise
# en: Stand Witness
title [307e88] = 做个见证
# en: {a} and {b} find you together, very nervous, holding hands. They've decided to make each other a promise, a proper one, at the lakeshore at dawn, the old way. They'd like a witness. "It has to be you," {a} says. "If you saw it, everyone will know it happened."
text [544a98] = {a}和{b}一起来找你，紧张得很，手牵着手。他们决定向彼此许下一个承诺，一个郑重的承诺，按老规矩，在黎明的湖岸边。他们想要一个见证人。“非你不可，”{a}说，“只要你看见了，大家就都会知道这是真的。”
# en: A lakeshore at dawn, two students holding hands on the shingle, a third student standing before them holding a lantern, mist on the water.
scene [8d845a] = 黎明时的湖岸，两个学生手牵手站在卵石滩上，第三个学生提着灯站在他们面前，水面上笼着薄雾。
# en: Make it a ceremony
opt.left.label [1a90b5] = 办成一场仪式
# en: You find the right words, short and true, and say them over the water as the sun comes up. {a} cries. {b} pretends not to. By breakfast the whole castle knows, and it's the happiest thing anyone has talked about all term.
opt.left.say.1 [1709c3] = 你找到了恰当的话，简短而真诚，在太阳升起时对着湖水说了出来。{a}哭了。{b}假装没哭。到早饭时整个城堡都知道了，这是这学期大家谈论过的最开心的事。
# en: You'd prepared a speech, and halfway through it you lose your place, and a duck lands in the shallows and starts shouting. {a} and {b} laugh so hard they have to hold each other up. It's perfect, they tell you. It is not what you planned.
opt.left.say.2 [31b580] = 你准备了一篇讲话，讲到一半忘了词，一只鸭子落在浅水里大叫起来。{a}和{b}笑得只能互相扶着。他们告诉你，这很完美。这可不是你计划的样子。
# en: Keep it quiet and simple
opt.right.label [6034ba] = 简简单单，悄悄进行
# en: "Just us, then," you say, and it is: three people and a lantern on the shingle, and the promise said plainly. Nobody else knows for a week. When it comes out, {a} and {b} only smile, because it was theirs first.
opt.right.say.1 [5aa39b] = “那就我们三个，”你说，事实也是如此：三个人，一盏灯，卵石滩上，承诺说得朴朴实实。一个星期都没别人知道。等消息传开，{a}和{b}只是笑笑，因为它先是属于他们的。

@event spot_flourish_named
# en: The {player}
title [68b19f] = “{player}式”
# en: There's a wand flourish going round the practice yards, a sort of showy twist before the cast, and everybody calls it "the {player}". You've never done it in your life. It does nothing at all for the spell. Every first-year in the castle is doing it.
text [64028f] = 练习场上流行起一种魔杖花式，施法前来一下花哨的转动，大家都管它叫“{player}式”。你这辈子从没这么做过。它对法术毫无用处。城堡里每个一年级生都在这么做。
# en: A practice yard full of students all flicking their wands in the same showy twist, one older student watching with arms folded.
scene [36dfc6] = 练习场上满是学生，人人都在用同一个花哨的动作甩着魔杖，一个年长的学生抱着胳膊在一旁看着。
# en: Teach them a flourish that helps
opt.left.label [635f42] = 教他们一个有用的花式
# en: You spend three afternoons in the practice yard showing them a real one, a small turn of the wrist that steadies the cast. It's much less showy. The first-years are disappointed, and then their spells start landing, and they call that one "the {player}" instead.
opt.left.say.1 [9bb90e] = 你花了三个下午在练习场上教他们一个真正的花式，手腕轻轻一转，能让施法更稳。它一点都不花哨。一年级生们很失望，然后他们的法术开始命中了，于是他们改叫这一个“{player}式”。
# en: Let them have it
opt.right.label [192387] = 随他们去
# en: A first-year doing "the {player}" too enthusiastically puts the twist through the greenhouse glass. Professor Thorne asks, in a voice like frost, whose name it is. Everybody looks at you.
opt.right.say.1 [14450f] = 一个一年级生太卖力地做“{player}式”，一下子把温室的玻璃打穿了。索恩教授用冰霜般的声音问，这是谁的名字。所有人都看向你。
# en: You let them have it. It's harmless, and it makes the practice yards look like a dance. Once, passing, you do it yourself, very solemnly, and the whole yard cheers.
opt.right.say.2 [e6b0c4] = 你随他们去了。这无伤大雅，还让练习场看起来像在跳舞。有一次路过时，你自己也郑重其事地做了一遍，整个练习场都欢呼起来。

@event spot_named_child
# en: A Baby Called {player}
title [7d6388] = 一个叫{player}的婴儿
# en: A letter from the village: the blacksmith's daughter has had a baby, and they've named it after you, and would you, if it's not too much, come to the naming? There'll be cake. The letter is spelled very carefully, as if it was copied out three times.
text [308be4] = 村里来了一封信：铁匠的女儿生了个孩子，用你的名字起了名，问你，如果不太麻烦的话，能不能来参加命名礼？会有蛋糕。信上的字写得非常仔细，像是誊抄了三遍。
# en: A cottage doorway in the village, a young couple holding a swaddled baby, an academy student on the step holding a small wrapped gift.
scene [b18bad] = 村里一间小屋的门口，一对年轻夫妇抱着襁褓中的婴儿，一个学院学生站在台阶上，手里拿着一份包好的小礼物。
# en: Go to the naming
opt.left.label [e03fad] = 去参加命名礼
# en: You go down the hill with a silver spoon you can't really afford. The baby is red and furious and has your name. You hold it for the whole naming and it falls asleep on you, which the village takes as a very good sign.
opt.left.say.1 [9f78a2] = 你带着一把自己其实买不起的银勺子下了山。婴儿红通通的，怒气冲冲，名字和你一样。整个命名礼你都抱着它，它在你怀里睡着了，全村人都认为这是个非常好的兆头。
# en: Send a gift and a letter
opt.right.label [dae1fc] = 寄一份礼物和一封信
# en: You can't get away, so you send a silver spoon and a long letter, to be read to the child when it's older, about the hill and the lake and the castle. The blacksmith's daughter writes back to say it's in a drawer, waiting.
opt.right.say.1 [5f5c8f] = 你走不开，于是寄去了一把银勺子和一封长信，等孩子长大了念给它听，信里写了山丘、湖和城堡。铁匠的女儿回信说，信放在抽屉里，等着。

@event spot_disguise_day
# en: An Ordinary Afternoon
title [07b5dc] = 一个普通的下午
# en: You haven't bought a loaf of bread in a month without someone asking you to sign it. Today you borrow a hat, a scarf and somebody's terrible coat, pull them all up, and walk down to the village to see if you can have one ordinary afternoon.
text [3db730] = 一个月来，你买一条面包都会有人请你在上面签名。今天你借了一顶帽子、一条围巾和不知谁的一件难看外套，全都拉得严严实实，走下山去村里，看看能不能过一个普通的下午。
# en: A student in a borrowed hat and a scarf pulled up to the nose walking through a busy village market, glancing sideways at the stalls.
scene [268506] = 一个戴着借来的帽子、围巾拉到鼻子的学生走过熙熙攘攘的村集，斜眼打量着摊位。
# en: Try it
opt.left.label [c589d7] = 试试看
# en: Nobody looks twice. You buy bread, argue with a stallholder over apples, sit on the quay and eat them, and walk back up the hill unrecognised, humming. It's the best afternoon you've had in a year.
opt.left.say.1 [e91e06] = 没人多看你一眼。你买了面包，和摊主为苹果讨价还价，坐在码头上吃完，哼着歌走回山上，谁也没认出你。这是你一年来过得最好的一个下午。
# en: You get as far as the bakery, where a small child points and says, very loudly, "That's {player} in a silly hat." The whole street turns. You sign eleven loaves. The coat, for some reason, becomes fashionable.
opt.left.say.2 [eba3bb] = 你只走到面包房，一个小孩就指着你大声说：“那是戴着傻帽子的{player}。”整条街都转过头来。你签了十一条面包。那件外套不知为什么流行了起来。
# en: Stay in the castle
opt.right.label [0285a8] = 待在城堡里
# en: You stay in, and read, and look out of the window at the village, very small and far away. Somebody brings you bread from it, which is kind of them. It isn't the same.
opt.right.say.1 [ac94fa] = 你待在城堡里读书，望着窗外的村子，那么小，那么远。有人从那里给你带了面包，真好心。可那不一样。

@event spot_higher_bar
# en: Because It's You
title [aa5435] = 因为是你
# en: Your essay comes back covered in red, with a mark lower than your neighbour's, though your neighbour's essay was, honestly, not as good. At the bottom the lecturer has written: "From anyone else, excellent. From you, I expect more. Everybody is watching what you do."
text [017810] = 你的论文发回来时满是红字，分数比你邻座的还低，可老实说，邻座的论文没你的好。讲师在最后写道：“换了别人，这是优秀。换了你，我期待更多。大家都在看你做什么。”
# en: A returned essay on a desk covered in red ink, a lecturer's note at the bottom, a student staring at it in disbelief beside a classmate's cleaner page.
scene [e6932c] = 书桌上摊着一篇发回来的论文，满是红墨水，末尾有讲师的批语，一个学生难以置信地盯着它，旁边是同学那页干净得多的卷子。
# en: Rise to it
opt.left.label [46df16] = 迎难而上
# en: You rewrite it from the first line, then rewrite the next one before it's due. It is the hardest you've worked all year. The lecturer's next note is one word, "Better", and you find you care about it more than any cheer in the Great Hall.
opt.left.say.1 [26b607] = 你从第一行开始重写，交下一篇之前又重写了一遍。这是你这一年里最拼命的一次。讲师的下一条批语只有一个词：“更好”，你发现自己在乎它，胜过大礼堂里的任何欢呼。
# en: Ask to be marked like anyone else
opt.right.label [3e7730] = 要求和别人一样评分
# en: You go to the lecturer's office and say, politely, that you'd like to be marked like everyone else. The lecturer looks at you over the spectacles, and then laughs, and changes the mark. "Fair," the lecturer says. "But I'll still be reading closely."
opt.right.say.1 [f24362] = 你去讲师办公室，礼貌地说你希望和大家一样评分。讲师从眼镜上方看着你，然后笑了，改了分数。“说得公道，”讲师说，“不过我还是会仔细读的。”
# en: You make your case. The lecturer hears you out and says that's exactly what a famous student would say. The mark stands. Worse, the story gets out, and for a week people call you "the one who argued about the mark".
opt.right.say.2 [615a06] = 你据理力争。讲师听完，说这正是一个出名的学生会说的话。分数不变。更糟的是，这件事传了出去，整整一个星期，人们都叫你“那个为分数争论的人”。

@event spot_history_essay
# en: A Living Figure
title [19bccb] = 一位在世人物
# en: A lecturer has set the second-years an essay on "a living figure in the academy's history", and forty of them have chosen you. They want interviews. There is a queue outside your door, with notebooks, and one of them has brought a list of a hundred questions.
text [d0b5b7] = 一位讲师给二年级生布置了一篇论文，题目是“学院历史上的一位在世人物”，其中四十个人选了你。他们想采访你。你门外排起了队，人人拿着笔记本，其中一个带来了一张有一百个问题的清单。
# en: A corridor outside a lecture hall, a queue of students holding notebooks and quills, one student at the front of it looking trapped.
scene [f0059f] = 阶梯教室外的走廊里，一排学生拿着笔记本和羽毛笔，排在最前面的那个学生一脸被困住的样子。
# en: Answer them all
opt.left.label [7d9608] = 每个问题都回答
# en: It takes a week of evenings. You answer every question, including the one about your favourite pudding and the one about whether you're afraid of dying. The essays are pinned up in the corridor. Two of them are better than anything you've written.
opt.left.say.1 [ba66b5] = 这花了一个星期的晚上。你回答了每一个问题，包括你最喜欢的布丁是什么，以及你怕不怕死。论文被钉在了走廊上。其中有两篇比你写过的任何东西都好。
# en: "I'm not history yet"
opt.right.label [b49fb7] = “我还不是历史”
# en: You tell them, cheerfully, that you're not history yet and might still do something stupid, and send them to the library to write about someone safely dead. Most of them laugh. The one with the hundred questions writes about you anyway.
opt.right.say.1 [b90439] = 你高高兴兴地告诉他们，你还不是历史，说不定还会干出什么蠢事，让他们去图书馆写某位已经稳稳当当去世的人。大多数人都笑了。那个带着一百个问题的人还是写了你。

@event spot_villain_play
# en: The Villain of Thornwick
title [c0387f] = 荆木的反派
# en: Some visiting Thornwick students are putting on a play in the village hall, and it's about you. In their version you are the villain: you cheat at duels, steal Thornwick's glory and have a moustache. The actor playing you hisses very well. A Lumenhold crowd has come along to boo.
text [f5302f] = 几个来访的荆木学生在村礼堂里演一出戏，讲的是你。在他们的版本里你是反派：决斗作弊，抢走荆木的荣光，还留着小胡子。演你的那个演员嘶嘶声做得非常好。一群流明堡的人专程去喝倒彩。
# en: A makeshift stage in a village hall, a student actor in a black cape and a cardboard crown hissing at the audience, another student in the back row laughing into a scarf.
scene [34afa2] = 村礼堂里搭起的简易舞台上，一个披黑斗篷、戴纸板王冠的学生演员朝观众嘶嘶作声，后排另一个学生把脸埋进围巾里笑。
# en: Go, and laugh loudest
opt.left.label [6c7ce1] = 去，笑得最响
# en: You laugh loudest, and the actor, spotting you, improvises a whole scene about your real lost duels, which turns out to be well researched. The village laughs at you, not with you. Thornwick's paper prints the best lines.
opt.left.say.1 [2d4566] = 你笑得最响，那个演员发现了你，即兴加了一整场关于你真实输掉的决斗的戏，看得出功课做得很足。全村人都在笑你，而不是和你一起笑。荆木的校报登出了最妙的台词。
# en: You sit in the front row and laugh loudest, and at the curtain the actor bows to you, and you bow back, and the hall cheers both of you. Afterwards you buy the cast supper. The moustache, you admit, is a fair cop.
opt.left.say.2 [a3c558] = 你坐在第一排，笑得最响，谢幕时演员向你鞠躬，你也回了一躬，全场为你们俩欢呼。散场后你请全体演员吃了晚饭。那撇小胡子，你承认，演得没毛病。
# en: Stay away
opt.right.label [91c94a] = 不去
# en: You stay away, which turns out to be the right thing, because the play without you is merely silly. Your friends come back with a programme and a very good impression of your hiss, which you have never once made.
opt.right.say.1 [dc62d7] = 你没去，结果这是对的，因为没有你在场，这出戏只是傻气而已。你的朋友们带回来一份节目单，还把你的嘶嘶声学得惟妙惟肖，而你一次都没那样嘶过。

@event spot_rival_lucky
# en: Just Lucky
title [4948f3] = 只是运气好
# en: {r} stops you in the corridor. "Everyone says you're a legend," {they} say{s}. "I think you're lucky. You were in the right place, at the right time, a lot. Someone should say it to your face." {r} doesn't look angry. {They} look{s} as if {they} had been waiting a long time to say it.
text [7e95e9] = {r}在走廊里拦住了你。“大家都说你是传奇，”{they}说，“我觉得你只是运气好。你很多次都恰好在对的时间出现在对的地方。总该有人当面告诉你。”{r}看起来并不生气。{They}看起来像是等着说这句话已经等了很久。
# en: A quiet corridor late in the day, two students facing each other, one leaning against the wall with arms folded, the other holding a stack of books.
scene [61f0d8] = 傍晚安静的走廊里，两个学生面对面，一个抱着胳膊靠在墙上，另一个抱着一摞书。
# en: Agree that a lot of it was luck
opt.left.label [eb16a8] = 承认很多都是运气
# en: "A lot of it was," you say. {r} blinks. You tell {r.them} which bits, honestly. By the end {r} is half laughing. "That's worse," {they} say{s}. "That's so much worse." It's the start of something like respect, on both sides.
opt.left.say.1 [bfec34] = “很多确实是，”你说。{r}眨了眨眼。你老老实实地告诉{r.them}是哪些部分。到最后{r}都快笑出来了。“那更糟，”{they}说，“糟多了。”这是一种类似敬意的东西的开始，双方都是。
# en: Tell {r} to try being lucky
opt.right.label [642855] = 让{r}也试试好运气
# en: "Try it," you say. "See how lucky it feels." {r} looks at you for a long moment and walks off. You win the argument. It doesn't feel like it, for the rest of the day.
opt.right.say.1 [141300] = “你试试，”你说，“看看这运气是什么滋味。”{r}看了你好一会儿，走开了。你赢了这场争论。那一整天，却没有赢了的感觉。

@event spot_guest_lecture
# en: Take the Class
title [5a6785] = 你来上这堂课
# en: A lecturer has asked you to take the first-years' class for an hour: "They listen to you. They certainly don't listen to me." Sixty first-years, the chalk, the lectern. The lecturer will be in the back row, taking notes on you.
text [c04288] = 一位讲师请你给一年级生上一小时的课：“他们听你的。他们肯定不听我的。”六十个一年级生，粉笔，讲台。讲师会坐在最后一排，记你的笔记。
# en: A lecture hall full of first-years in rows, an older student at the lectern holding chalk, a professor sitting in the back row with a notebook.
scene [0d6928] = 阶梯教室里坐满了一排排一年级生，一个年长的学生拿着粉笔站在讲台边，一位教授坐在最后一排拿着笔记本。
# en: Teach it properly
opt.left.label [ef9064] = 好好讲
# en: You teach them the thing you found hardest, the way you wish someone had taught you, and they get it. All sixty. The lecturer comes down at the end, looks at the board for a long time, and copies it into the notebook.
opt.left.say.1 [ce2ad2] = 你教了他们你当初觉得最难的东西，用你希望当年有人教你的方式，他们听懂了。六十个人全懂了。最后讲师走下来，盯着黑板看了很久，把它抄进了笔记本。
# en: You start well, and lose them at the second diagram, and never get them back. By the end the first-years are passing notes about your hair. The lecturer takes the last ten minutes and says, kindly, that teaching is harder than it looks.
opt.left.say.2 [12af10] = 你开头不错，到第二张图就把他们讲丢了，再也没找回来。到最后，一年级生们在传纸条讨论你的发型。讲师接过了最后十分钟，和气地说，教书比看起来难。
# en: Tell stories instead
opt.right.label [5ac9cd] = 改讲故事
# en: You put the chalk down and tell them stories: the lake, the stairs, the first time a spell went right. They hang on every word. They learn almost nothing about the syllabus. A few of them, you suspect, will stay up late tonight practising.
opt.right.say.1 [8a1ba3] = 你放下粉笔，给他们讲故事：湖，楼梯，第一次法术成功的那一刻。他们听得入了迷。关于课程大纲，他们几乎什么也没学到。你猜，他们当中有几个今晚会熬夜练习。

@event spot_legend_burden
# en: A Legend Can't Miss
title [d74310] = 传奇不能失手
# en: In practice, in front of the class, an easy spell goes wrong in your hands. It just fizzles, the way spells do for everyone, now and then. But the whole room gasps as if the sky had fallen, and you can feel them all waiting to see what you'll do.
text [031d02] = 练习课上，当着全班的面，一个简单的法术在你手里出了岔子。只是熄了火，就像每个人的法术偶尔都会的那样。可整个房间倒抽一口凉气，仿佛天塌了下来，你能感觉到他们都在等着看你会怎么做。
# en: A practice room, a student's spell fizzling into smoke, a whole class turned to stare in shocked silence.
scene [6caa23] = 练习室里，一个学生的法术化作一缕青烟熄灭，全班转过头来，震惊地一片寂静。
# en: Laugh, and try again
opt.left.label [3c9cce] = 笑一笑，再来一次
# en: You laugh, say "well, that's a relief", and try again, and get it. The room laughs with you. Afterwards a first-year says it was the best thing they'd seen all year: that even you miss. You decide that's worth something.
opt.left.say.1 [223845] = 你笑了，说了句“这下放心了”，又试了一次，成功了。全屋的人跟着你笑了。事后一个一年级生说，这是自己这一年里见过最棒的事：原来连你也会失手。你觉得这还挺有价值的。
# en: Practise until it never happens again
opt.right.label [e159f6] = 练到再也不会出错
# en: You stay behind and cast it a hundred times, until your arm aches and it couldn't fail if it tried. It never happens again, in front of anyone. Sometimes you wish it would, so you could stop being so careful.
opt.right.say.1 [00b7a2] = 你留下来把它施放了一百遍，直到胳膊发酸，想失败都难。在任何人面前，它再也没出过错。有时你倒希望它出一次错，这样你就不用那么小心翼翼了。

@event renown_fallen_entry
# en: Yesterday's News
title [7759f8] = 昨日新闻
# en: You notice it on a Tuesday. You walk the length of the long corridor and nobody turns. In the common room, a copy of the Ledger with your face on the front is being used to light the fire. Not long ago you couldn't buy bread without signing it. Now you're just somebody who used to be somebody.
text [a82872] = 你是在一个星期二注意到的。你走完了整条长廊，没有一个人转头。公共休息室里，一份头版印着你的脸的《纪事报》正被拿来生火。不久之前，你买条面包都得签名。现在你只是一个曾经是个人物的人。
# en: A student walking through a busy corridor that doesn't part for them, a crumpled old copy of the Ledger with their face on it lying in a bin.
scene [42e691] = 一个学生走过熙熙攘攘的走廊，人群不再为这个学生让路，一份印着这个学生的脸的旧《纪事报》皱巴巴地躺在垃圾桶里。
# en: Enjoy the quiet
opt.left.label [293287] = 享受这份清静
# en: It's wonderful, mostly. You eat your lunch where you like. You walk to the lake without an escort. Now and then somebody squints at you and says "weren't you...?", and you say "no", and they believe you.
opt.left.say.1 [3b08a9] = 大部分时候，这感觉好极了。你想在哪儿吃午饭就在哪儿吃。你走去湖边，不再有人簇拥。偶尔有人眯着眼睛看你说“你不是那个……？”，你说“不是”，他们就信了。
# en: Plan the comeback
opt.right.label [b51854] = 策划东山再起
# en: You sit down with a sheet of paper and write a list of everything that made people talk. Some of it you'd do again. Some of it you can't believe you did. You fold the paper very small and keep it in your pocket, just in case.
opt.right.say.1 [cf426e] = 你坐下来，拿出一张纸，列出所有曾让人们议论的事。有些你会再做一次。有些你简直不敢相信自己做过。你把那张纸折得很小，揣在口袋里，以防万一。

@event fallen_old_fan
# en: The Fan Who Stayed
title [8193c3] = 留下来的那个粉丝
# en: Most of the first-years who used to queue for you have found somebody else. But one of them, older now, stops you in the courtyard and says, a bit shyly, "I still think you were the best one. For what it's worth." {chosen(spot_fan_charm, left): You recognise the blue thread on their wrist.|They've still got your name written inside the cover of a book.}
text [fec067] = 曾经为你排队的一年级生大多另找了别人。但其中一个，如今大了些，在庭院里拦住你，有点害羞地说：“我还是觉得你是最好的那个。不管这话值多少。”{chosen(spot_fan_charm, left): 你认出了对方手腕上的蓝线。|对方的一本书封皮里还写着你的名字。}
# en: A first-year holding a frayed charm on a ribbon, standing a little awkwardly in front of an older student in a quiet courtyard.
scene [a37d87] = 安静的庭院里，一个一年级生手里攥着一个系在缎带上、已经磨毛的护身符，有点局促地站在一个年长的学生面前。
# en: Ask how they're getting on
opt.left.label [499c6b] = 问问对方过得怎么样
# en: You ask about their lectures, their friends, the spell they couldn't get. They tell you everything. It turns out they've been doing very well, and nobody has asked. You walk them to supper and they talk the whole way.
opt.left.say.1 [721e5a] = 你问起对方的课、朋友，还有那个怎么也学不会的法术。对方什么都告诉了你。原来对方一直过得很好，只是没人问过。你陪对方走去吃晚饭，一路上对方说个不停。
# en: Thank them, and keep walking
opt.right.label [d12fa6] = 道声谢，继续走
# en: "That's very kind," you say, and mean it, and keep walking, because you're late. It's only later that you realise they'd wanted to say more. The next time you look for them, they're with a crowd of their own.
opt.right.say.1 [59175f] = “你真好，”你说，也是真心的，然后继续走了，因为你要迟到了。直到后来你才意识到，对方还想多说几句。下一次你去找对方时，对方已经和自己的一群朋友在一起了。

@event fallen_where_now
# en: Where Are They Now?
title [612d31] = 他们现在在哪儿？
# en: Nessa wants to run a piece: "Where Are They Now?", a look back at the academy's brightest stars, starring you. "It's not unkind," she says. "It's nostalgic. People love a comeback. Or a cautionary tale. You can choose which."
text [83d76b] = 内莎想做一篇报道：《他们现在在哪儿？》，回顾学院最耀眼的明星，主角是你。“不是刻薄的那种，”她说，“是怀旧的。人们爱看东山再起。或者警世故事。你可以选哪一种。”
# en: A newspaper office at dusk, an editor with a quill leaning across a desk toward a student who has their arms folded.
scene [89dba4] = 黄昏时的报社办公室，一位编辑握着羽毛笔隔着桌子探身向一个抱着胳膊的学生。
# en: Give her the comeback
opt.left.label [2a8b4b] = 给她一个东山再起的故事
# en: You give her a new plan and an old smile, and the piece runs on the front page: "{player}: not done yet". By the end of the week people are turning in corridors again, cautiously, as if they'd forgotten how.
opt.left.say.1 [f17c98] = 你给了她一个新计划和一个老笑容，报道登上了头版：《{player}：尚未落幕》。到周末，走廊里的人又开始转头了，小心翼翼的，像是忘了该怎么转。
# en: You try to sound like someone with a plan. It comes out sounding like someone with a list. The piece runs on page six, between the weather and the crossword, under the heading "fading gracefully?"
opt.left.say.2 [666bee] = 你努力让自己听起来像个有计划的人。结果听起来像个有清单的人。报道登在第六版，夹在天气和填字游戏之间，标题是《体面地淡出？》。
# en: Ask her to leave you out
opt.right.label [f31056] = 请她别写你
# en: "Leave me out of it," you say. "I like it here, out of the paper." Nessa looks at you for a long moment and puts her quill down. "That," she says, "is the most interesting thing you've ever told me. Fine."
opt.right.say.1 [830cd9] = “别写我了，”你说，“我喜欢待在报纸外面。”内莎看了你好一会儿，放下了羽毛笔。“这个，”她说，“是你告诉过我的最有意思的事。好吧。”

@event fallen_unrecognised
# en: Just Another Student
title [0b100f] = 又一个普通学生
# en: In the village, the baker who used to lean out of the window and shout your name sells you a loaf, gives you your change and says "next, please" without looking up. Outside, the children who used to follow you are following somebody else, over by the fountain.
text [f9f06d] = 在村里，那个曾经从窗口探出身来喊你名字的面包师卖给你一条面包，找了零钱，头也不抬地说“下一位”。外面，以前跟着你的孩子们正跟着另一个人，在喷泉那边。
# en: A village bakery counter, a baker handing a loaf to a student without a second glance, a short queue behind.
scene [4502e2] = 村里面包房的柜台前，面包师看都没看就把一条面包递给一个学生，身后排着一小队人。
# en: Laugh about it
opt.left.label [850c3c] = 一笑置之
# en: You laugh, out loud, in the street, and a dog barks at you. You eat the end of the loaf on the quay and watch the boats, and nobody comes to ask you anything at all, and it's lovely.
opt.left.say.1 [0a059f] = 你在街上笑出了声，一条狗冲你叫了起来。你坐在码头上吃完面包的最后一截，看着船，没有一个人来问你任何事，这真好。
# en: Remind the baker who you are
opt.right.label [8f8693] = 提醒面包师你是谁
# en: "It's me," you say. "From the Ledger." The baker squints, says "oh, yes", and gives you a bun, out of pity. You eat it on the way back up the hill, slowly. It tastes of nothing much.
opt.right.say.1 [c72fab] = “是我啊，”你说，“《纪事报》上那个。”面包师眯着眼睛说“哦，对”，出于同情给了你一个小面包。你在回山上的路上慢慢吃着。没什么味道。

@event fallen_mocked
# en: Weren't You Somebody?
title [3185a3] = 你以前不是挺有名的吗？
# en: At lunch, someone at the next table says, just loud enough, "Weren't you somebody once?" The table laughs. A couple of people at your own table laugh too, then look as if they wish they hadn't. Everyone waits to see whether you'll answer.
text [fda369] = 午饭时，邻桌有人刚好用能让人听见的声音说：“你以前不是挺有名的吗？”那一桌笑了起来。你们这桌也有几个人笑了，随即又一副后悔的样子。所有人都在等着看你会不会回答。
# en: A crowded refectory, a student at the next table pointing with a fork, a ripple of laughter, one student sitting very straight with their tray.
scene [32f73e] = 拥挤的膳堂里，邻桌一个学生用叉子指着这边，笑声一阵阵传开，一个学生端着托盘坐得笔直。
# en: Answer with a joke
opt.left.label [5a4c5d] = 用玩笑回应
# en: "I was," you say. "Then I got hungry." You hold up your pudding. The whole refectory laughs, at the right person this time, and somebody at the next table actually applauds.
opt.left.say.1 [3dcdbb] = “是啊，”你说，“后来我饿了。”你举起你的布丁。整个膳堂都笑了，这一次笑对了人，邻桌甚至有人鼓起了掌。
# en: Your joke comes out a beat too late and a little too sharp. Nobody laughs. The next table goes back to its lunch, satisfied. You eat yours very quickly.
opt.left.say.2 [89d4a7] = 你的玩笑慢了半拍，也太尖刻了一点。没人笑。邻桌心满意足地继续吃饭。你飞快地吃完了你的。
# en: Say nothing, and eat your lunch
opt.right.label [01cc6f] = 一言不发，吃你的饭
# en: You say nothing and eat your lunch, and in a minute they've moved on to something else. A friend at your table kicks you gently under it and passes you their pudding. You take it.
opt.right.say.1 [dd1a35] = 你什么也没说，吃你的午饭，没过一分钟他们就转到别的话题上了。你们这桌的一个朋友在桌子底下轻轻踢了你一下，把自己的布丁递给了你。你收下了。

@event fallen_friends
# en: The Ones Who Stayed
title [5337c3] = 留下来的人
# en: The invitations have stopped. The crowd has gone. But on Thursday night, as always, {f} has pulled out the chair in the corner of the common room, and the cards are dealt, and the cocoa is going cold. "You're late," {f} says. "We waited."
text [eca721] = 邀请停了。人群散了。可到了星期四晚上，像往常一样，{f}在公共休息室的角落里拉开了那把椅子，牌已经发好，可可正在变凉。“你来晚了，”{f}说，“我们一直在等你。”
# en: A small group of students around a table in a quiet corner of a common room late at night, cards scattered, cups of cocoa, one chair pulled out for someone arriving.
scene [4451aa] = 深夜公共休息室安静的角落里，一小群学生围坐在桌旁，纸牌散落，可可杯摆着，一把椅子被拉开，等着某个人到来。
# en: Sit down and stay late
opt.left.label [58598f] = 坐下，待到很晚
# en: You sit down and stay until the fire goes out. Nobody asks you about anything you did. They ask what you think, about everything, and you realise you'd almost forgotten anybody wanted to know.
opt.left.say.1 [f95abc] = 你坐下来，一直待到炉火熄灭。没人问你做过什么。他们问你怎么看，什么都问，你这才意识到，你几乎忘了还有人想知道你的想法。
# en: Say you've been a poor friend
opt.right.label [5ba912] = 说你最近是个差劲的朋友
# en: "I've been a poor friend," you say. {f} shrugs. "You were busy being famous." Then, more quietly: "You're back now." {f} deals you in, and you lose, as usual, and it's the best evening in months.
opt.right.say.1 [5034e4] = “我最近是个差劲的朋友，”你说。{f}耸了耸肩。“你忙着出名呢。”然后，声音低了些：“现在你回来了。”{f}给你发了牌，你照旧输了，这是几个月来最好的一个晚上。
